```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>",
  message = FALSE,
  warning = FALSE,
  out.width = "100%"
)
# light gif for the vignette: smaller width (magick) to keep the package small
light_gif <- function(from, to, width = 700){
  dir.create(dirname(to), showWarnings = FALSE)
  ok <- tryCatch({
    magick::image_read(from) |>
      magick::image_scale(as.character(width)) |>
      magick::image_write(to, format = "gif")
    TRUE
  }, error = function(e) file.copy(from, to, overwrite = TRUE))
  invisible(ok)
}
show_fig <- function(f) if(file.exists(f)) knitr::include_graphics(f) else invisible(NULL)
```

[Versión en español](mapas_animados.html)

## Introduction

An animated map shows **how transmission moves** week by week: where it starts, where it spreads and when it fades. **densnv** has two functions:

```{r functions, echo = FALSE}
knitr::kable(data.frame(
  "function" = c("mp_animated_loc()", "mp_animated_mpo()"),
  unit = c("Locality (proportional circles)", "Municipality (circles or choropleth)"),
  cases = c("Probable, confirmed, final diagnosis or serotype; one or several at once",
            "Confirmed (DNG, DCSA and DG)"),
  engine = c("ggplot2 + gifski, with optional epidemic curve", "tmap + gifski"),
  check.names = FALSE))
```

Titles and labels are in Spanish, the language of the users of the Mexican vector-borne disease program.

```{r setup}
library(densnv)
```

## Data

```{r data, eval = FALSE}
path <- "DENGUE2_.txt"
x <- data.table::fread(path, quote = "", fill = TRUE)
```

```{r data_local, include = FALSE}
# local path (not shown in the vignette); set the current year
path <- "/Users/fdzul/Library/CloudStorage/Dropbox/dataset/dge/denv/2026/DENGUE2_.txt"
x <- data.table::fread(path, quote = "", fill = TRUE)
year <- max(x$ANO, na.rm = TRUE)
# only the last 16 weeks are animated in the vignette (lighter gifs)
weeks <- max(x$SEM[x$ANO == year], na.rm = TRUE) - 15:0
```

## By locality: `mp_animated_loc()`

It counts the cases by locality of report and epidemiological week, and draws them as proportional circles over the state (country) or municipal (state) boundaries. The size and colour scales are **the same in every frame**, so the weeks can be compared. With `curve = TRUE` it adds the epidemic curve below, with the current week marked.

### One state: Oaxaca

```{r loc_oaxaca, eval = FALSE}
mp_animated_loc(path = path,
                snv_dataset = "DENV",
                year = 2026,
                week = 1:52,
                var = "probable",
                multi = "sum",
                country = FALSE,
                cve_edo = "20",
                breaks = NULL,
                pal = "-viridis",
                text_size = 0.4,
                fps = 2,
                curve = TRUE,
                map_legend = TRUE,
                legend_title = "Casos por localidad",
                save = TRUE,
                name = "dengue_oaxaca_2026",
                dir = "./")
```

```{r loc_oaxaca_gif, include = FALSE}
f <- "figures/anim-loc-oaxaca.gif"
if(!file.exists(f)){
  g <- mp_animated_loc(path = path, snv_dataset = "DENV", year = year,
                       week = weeks, var = "probable", multi = "sum",
                       country = FALSE, cve_edo = "20", curve = TRUE,
                       height = 1200, save = FALSE)
  light_gif(g, f)
}
```

```{r loc_oaxaca_fig, echo = FALSE}
show_fig("figures/anim-loc-oaxaca.gif")
```

### Several types of cases

The `var` argument takes one or several values: `"probable"`, `"confirmado"`, `"DNG"`, `"DCSA"`, `"DG"`, `"DENV1"` to `"DENV4"`. With several, `multi` sets how they are combined:

1. **`multi = "sum"`:** adds them in a single map (e.g. the severe forms, `c("DCSA", "DG")`).
2. **`multi = "facet"`:** one panel per value, with the same scale (e.g. one panel per serotype). A case can appear in more than one panel.

```{r loc_serotypes, eval = FALSE}
mp_animated_loc(path = path,
                snv_dataset = "DENV",
                year = 2026,
                week = 1:52,
                var = c("DENV1", "DENV2", "DENV3", "DENV4"),
                multi = "facet",
                ncol = 2,
                country = TRUE,
                cve_edo = NULL,
                curve = TRUE,
                save = TRUE,
                name = "serotypes_mexico_2026",
                dir = "./")
```

```{r loc_serotypes_gif, include = FALSE}
f <- "figures/anim-loc-serotipos.gif"
if(!file.exists(f)){
  g <- mp_animated_loc(path = path, snv_dataset = "DENV", year = year,
                       week = weeks, var = c("DENV1", "DENV2", "DENV3", "DENV4"),
                       multi = "facet", ncol = 2, country = TRUE, curve = TRUE,
                       height = 1400, save = FALSE)
  light_gif(g, f)
}
```

```{r loc_serotypes_fig, echo = FALSE}
show_fig("figures/anim-loc-serotipos.gif")
```

## By municipality: `mp_animated_mpo()`

It counts the confirmed cases (non-severe dengue, with warning signs and severe) by municipality of residence and week. With `dot = TRUE` it draws proportional circles; with `dot = FALSE`, a choropleth map with the palette `pal`.

```{r mpo_oaxaca, eval = FALSE}
mp_animated_mpo(data = x,
                year = 2026,
                name = "Oaxaca",
                dir = "./",
                country = FALSE,
                breaks = NULL,
                save = TRUE,
                cve_edo = "20",
                text_size = 0.3,
                dot = TRUE,
                pal = viridis::viridis)
```

```{r mpo_oaxaca_gif, include = FALSE}
f <- "figures/anim-mpo-oaxaca.gif"
if(!file.exists(f)){
  tmp <- paste0(tempdir(), "/")
  mp_animated_mpo(data = x[x$SEM %in% weeks, ], year = year, name = "oaxaca",
                  dir = tmp, country = FALSE, save = TRUE, cve_edo = "20",
                  text_size = 0.3, dot = TRUE, pal = viridis::viridis)
  light_gif(paste0(tmp, "oaxaca_dot_animated_map.gif"), f)
}
```

```{r mpo_oaxaca_fig, echo = FALSE}
show_fig("figures/anim-mpo-oaxaca.gif")
```

## Recommendations

1. **Size of the gif:** with 52 weeks and the whole country, the gif can weigh several MB. For presentations use `fps = 2` or `3` and limit `week` to the period of interest.
2. **Comparing weeks:** the scales are the same in every frame, so a circle of the same size means the same number of cases in any week.
3. **Saving:** with `save = TRUE` the gif is saved in `dir`; with `save = FALSE` it is only shown in the RStudio Viewer.
4. The gifs of this vignette show only the last 16 weeks and are reduced in size.

## Notes

1. `mp_animated_loc()` uses the locality of **report** and the INEGI 2020 locality catalogue (`rgeomex::iter_2020_loc`).
2. `mp_animated_mpo()` uses the municipality of **residence** and excludes the cases from other countries.
3. Both functions need the gifski package.
